64BitAllocator: pick allocation addresses at HOST page granularity, not guest.

Fixes i386 guests, which before this patch ran correctly only about two times in five on a 16 KiB host and
otherwise killed FEXInterpreter with a SIGSEGV.

64BitAllocator is the allocator FEX installs when the guest is 32-bit — its job is to take the host's
UPPER address space so the guest can own the low 4 GB (`InitAllocator(Loader.Is64BitMode())`). Only 32-bit
guests take this path, which is why the large-host-page work in patches/0001 never touched it: only x86_64
guests were ever run against that patch.

It tracks a per-page bitmap indexed in GUEST pages, then hands the chosen index to mmap with MAP_FIXED
forced on:

    AllocatedOffset = Region->SlabInfo->Base + AllocatedPage * FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE;
    ::mmap((void*)AllocatedOffset, length, prot, (flags & ~MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE) | MAP_FIXED, fd, offset);

A MAP_FIXED whose address is not host-page-aligned fails with EINVAL, so on a 16 KiB host three of every
four page indices are unusable. FEX does not treat that mmap as fatal; it carries on and then faults
walking into the PROT_NONE tail of its own reservation. Captured with
`strace -f -e trace=mmap,mprotect`:

    mmap(0x7ffff67c4000, 159629312, PROT_NONE, ...MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE) = 0x7ffff67c4000   reserve, aligned
    mprotect(0x7ffff67c4000, 12288, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE) = 0                             commit 3 x 4 KiB
    mmap(0x7ffff67c7000, 16384, RWX, ...MAP_FIXED)        = -1 EINVAL                     base+12288
    --- SIGSEGV {si_code=SEGV_ACCERR, si_addr=0x7ffff67c8000} ---

Which index comes up depends on ASLR, hence the flakiness; with `setarch -R` it fails every time.

The fix keeps the bitmap in guest pages — nothing about its layout, the `alignas` on the bitset or the
`static_assert(sizeof(LiveVMARegion) == FEX_PAGE_SIZE)` changes — and only makes the ADDRESSES it hands
out host-page-aligned:

  * round `length` up to the host page, so an allocation owns whole host pages and the next one cannot
    start inside a page this one already holds;
  * ask the free-range scan for `PagesPerHostPage - 1` extra pages, then round the found start up. The
    slack is what makes the rounding safe: the start moves by less than the slack, so the range actually
    mapped is still wholly inside the free run the scan found.

A caller-supplied StartingPosition is left exactly alone: that path is MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, the caller
compares the result against the address it asked for, and a host-aligned request rounds to itself anyway.

Verified on a 16 KiB aarch64 host with static test guests (emulators/fex-linux/tests/mk_hello.py): i386
and x86_64 both run correctly and repeatably, where i386 was ~2/5 before.

--- a/FEXCore/Source/Utils/Allocator/64BitAllocator.cpp
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Utils/Allocator/64BitAllocator.cpp
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
 #include <sys/mman.h>
 #include <sys/utsname.h>
 #include <sys/user.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
 #include <type_traits>
 #include <utility>

@@ -34,6 +35,20 @@

 thread_local FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* TLSThread {};

+// The size of a page as the KERNEL sees it, which is not FEX_PAGE_SIZE.
+//
+// FEX_PAGE_SIZE is the GUEST page size and is correctly 4 KiB for an x86 guest. This allocator, however,
+// hands out addresses that it then mmap()s with MAP_FIXED, so its granularity has to be the HOST's — a
+// MAP_FIXED request whose address is not host-page-aligned fails with EINVAL, which on a 16 KiB host
+// rejects three out of every four otherwise-valid page indices.
+static size_t HostPageSize() {
+  static const size_t Size = [] {
+    const long Result = ::sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE);
+    return Result > 0 ? static_cast<size_t>(Result) : static_cast<size_t>(FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE);
+  }();
+  return Size;
+}
+
 class OSAllocator_64Bit final : public Alloc::HostAllocator {
 public:
   OSAllocator_64Bit();
@@ -257,7 +272,9 @@
   }

   bool Fixed = (flags & MAP_FIXED) || (flags & MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE);
-  length = FEXCore::AlignUp(length, FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE);
+  // Whole host pages: a mapping is the kernel's minimum unit, so rounding to the guest page here would
+  // leave the next allocation starting inside a page this one already owns.
+  length = FEXCore::AlignUp(length, HostPageSize());

   uint64_t AddrEnd = Addr + length;
   size_t NumberOfPages = length / FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SIZE;
@@ -284,6 +301,15 @@
     uint64_t AllocatedPage {~0ULL};
     uint64_t NumberOfPages = length >> FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SHIFT;

+    // The used-page bitmap is indexed in GUEST pages and stays that way — but the address this lambda
+    // picks is passed straight to mmap(MAP_FIXED), so the START has to be host-page-aligned. Ask the scan
+    // for enough extra pages to absorb that alignment, so that after rounding the start up the requested
+    // range is still wholly inside the free run the scan found. With a caller-supplied StartingPosition
+    // there is nothing to choose: the caller wants that exact address (MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE checks the
+    // result), and a host-aligned one rounds to itself anyway.
+    const uint64_t PagesPerHostPage = HostPageSize() >> FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SHIFT;
+    const uint64_t ScanPages = NumberOfPages + (StartingPosition ? 0 : PagesPerHostPage - 1);
+
     if (Region->FreeSpace >= length) {
       uint64_t LastAllocation =
         StartingPosition ? (StartingPosition - Region->SlabInfo->Base) >> FEXCore::Utils::FEX_PAGE_SHIFT : Region->LastPageAllocation;
